DATA.writing = {
eng:{
B1:{essay:["Write about a machine or invention that is important in your life. Say what it is, how you use it and why it is important. (about 100 words)","The most important machine in my life is my car. It is not new, but it works well. I use it every day to go to work, because my office is thirty kilometres from my house and there are no buses in the morning. At the weekend I use it to visit my family and to go shopping.\n\nMy car is important because it gives me freedom. I can go where I want, when I want. Without it, I would need two hours to get to work. However, petrol is expensive, so in the future I would like to buy an electric car."],
task:["email","You work in a factory. Write an email to your boss to say that a machine is broken. Explain what happened and what you did. (about 80 words)","Subject: Problem with machine 4\n\nDear Mr Brown,\n\nI am writing to tell you that machine 4 stopped working this morning at ten o'clock. It made a loud noise and then a red light appeared.\n\nI turned the machine off and I called the maintenance team. They will come tomorrow at eight. Until then, we are using machine 2.\n\nPlease tell me if you need more information.\n\nBest regards,\nAlex"]},
B2:{essay:["'Technology has made our lives easier but not better.' Do you agree? (about 150 words)","It is often said that technology has made life easier without really improving it. In my opinion, this is only partly true.\n\nOn the one hand, it is undeniable that technology saves us time and effort. We can pay bills, buy food and talk to people on the other side of the world without leaving the sofa. Moreover, modern medicine allows us to live longer and healthier lives.\n\nOn the other hand, an easier life is not always a happier one. Many people feel more stressed than before because they are connected to work all day. In addition, we spend so much time in front of screens that we see our friends less often.\n\nTo sum up, I believe technology has improved our lives in many ways, but we need to learn to use it sensibly. The problem is not the tool, but how we use it."],
task:["report","Your manager has asked you to write a report on a new piece of equipment you tested. Describe it, give its advantages and disadvantages, and make a recommendation. (about 150 words)","Report on the X200 cutting machine\n\nIntroduction\nThe aim of this report is to evaluate the X200 cutting machine, which was tested in our workshop for two weeks.\n\nAdvantages\nThe machine is noticeably faster than our current model: it cut 120 parts per hour, compared with 90. It is also quieter and easier to clean. Operators learned to use it in less than a day.\n\nDisadvantages\nThe main drawback is the price, which is 30% higher than similar machines. In addition, spare parts can only be ordered from the manufacturer, and delivery takes about a week.\n\nRecommendation\nIn spite of the cost, I would recommend buying the X200, since the increase in production would pay for the difference in less than two years. However, I suggest keeping a stock of the most common spare parts."]},
C1:{essay:["Some people think engineers should focus only on technical excellence; others believe they must also consider the social and environmental impact of their work. Discuss both views and give your own opinion. (about 200 words)","Engineering has traditionally been judged by a simple standard: does it work? Increasingly, however, engineers are expected to answer a second question, namely what their work does to society and the environment.\n\nThose who favour a purely technical focus argue that specialisation is what makes engineers valuable. A structural engineer is trained to calculate loads, not to judge whether a shopping centre is good for a town. According to this view, decisions of that kind belong to politicians and the public.\n\nNevertheless, this position has clear limits. Engineers are often the only people who fully understand the consequences of a design, from the energy it will consume to the waste it will leave behind. To remain silent on these matters is, in effect, to make a choice. Furthermore, many environmental problems are far cheaper to prevent at the design stage than to correct afterwards.\n\nIn my view, the two approaches are not really in conflict. Considering the wider impact of a project is itself a mark of technical excellence, since a solution that creates new problems is not a good solution. Engineers need not make every decision, but they do have a duty to ensure that those who decide are properly informed."],
task:["proposal","Your company wants to reduce energy consumption in its factory. Write a proposal for your director suggesting two or three measures and explaining their benefits. (about 200 words)","Proposal: reducing energy consumption at the plant\n\nIntroduction\nThis proposal outlines three measures to reduce our energy consumption, which rose by 12% last year, and explains the benefits of each.\n\nLighting\nThe production hall is still lit by fluorescent tubes that remain on throughout the day. Replacing them with LED fittings controlled by presence sensors would cut lighting costs by roughly 60% and would pay for itself within eighteen months.\n\nCompressed air\nAn inspection last month revealed numerous leaks in the compressed air system. Repairing them is inexpensive and, according to the supplier, could save up to a fifth of the energy the compressors use.\n\nSolar panels\nThe roof of the warehouse offers 2,000 square metres of unused space. A photovoltaic installation there could cover about a third of our daytime demand. Although this is the most expensive measure, subsidies are currently available.\n\nConclusion\nI recommend starting immediately with the first two measures, which require little investment, and commissioning a feasibility study for the third. Taken together, they would reduce both our costs and our carbon footprint significantly."]},
C2:{essay:["'The greatest engineering challenges of this century are not technical but political.' To what extent do you agree? (about 220 words)","It has become commonplace to observe that we already possess most of the technology required to address the great problems of our time. Clean energy can be generated at competitive prices, water can be purified and cities can be made resilient to floods. Why, then, does progress remain so painfully slow?\n\nThe answer, I would argue, lies largely outside the laboratory. A wind farm can be designed in months, yet the permits needed to connect it may take a decade. A flood barrier is a tractable problem in hydraulics; deciding whose land it will occupy, and who will pay, is not. In case after case, the limiting factor turns out to be our capacity to reach collective decisions rather than our ingenuity.\n\nIt would nevertheless be a mistake to dismiss the technical dimension altogether. Long-term energy storage, to take one example, remains genuinely unsolved, and no amount of political will can substitute for a breakthrough that has yet to occur. Moreover, the distinction is less clear-cut than it appears, since cheaper technology has a habit of dissolving political obstacles that once seemed immovable.\n\nOn balance, I broadly agree with the statement, with one reservation. The challenges are political in the sense that politics is where they are currently stuck. Engineers who wish to make a difference would therefore do well to learn the art of persuasion alongside that of calculation."],
task:["review","Write a review for a professional magazine of a book, documentary or tool related to engineering or technology. Evaluate its strengths and weaknesses and say who would benefit from it. (about 200 words)","'To Engineer Is Human' by Henry Petroski\n\nFew books about engineering manage to hold the attention of the general reader, and fewer still can be said to have changed how professionals think about their own work. Henry Petroski's classic does both.\n\nIts central claim is deceptively simple: engineering advances through failure. Every collapsed bridge and cracked fuselage, Petroski argues, teaches lessons that success never could, because a structure that stands tells us only that it was strong enough, not by how much. The case studies, from the Tacoma Narrows bridge to the walkways of a Kansas City hotel, are recounted with admirable clarity.\n\nThe book is not without its flaws. Written in the 1980s, it inevitably feels dated when it turns to computers, and readers hoping for mathematical detail will be disappointed. Some chapters also labour their point rather longer than necessary.\n\nThese are minor reservations, however. What endures is the author's humane insistence that error is not a scandal to be hidden but the raw material of progress. I would recommend it without hesitation to students, who will find in it a welcome antidote to the false certainty of textbooks, and to experienced engineers in need of a little humility."]}},
elec:{
B1:{essay:["How can people save electricity at home? Give three ideas and explain them. (about 100 words)","Electricity is expensive, so it is important to save it. Here are three easy ideas.\n\nFirst, we should turn off the lights when we leave a room. Many people forget, and the lights stay on for hours.\n\nSecond, we can use LED bulbs. They cost a little more, but they use much less energy and they last longer.\n\nThird, we should not leave machines like the TV or the computer on at night. They use electricity all the time.\n\nIn conclusion, if we change these small habits, we will pay less and we will also help the planet."],
task:["email","There was a power cut in your building last night. Write an email to your landlord. Say what happened and ask him to check the installation. (about 80 words)","Subject: Power cut in flat 3B\n\nDear Mr Wilson,\n\nI am writing because there was a power cut in my flat last night. It happened at about nine o'clock, when I was using the oven and the washing machine. The power came back after I switched off the oven.\n\nThis is the third time this month. I think the installation is very old. Could you send an electrician to check it, please?\n\nThank you for your help.\n\nKind regards,\nAlex"]},
B2:{essay:["'Every new building should be obliged to have solar panels.' Do you agree? (about 150 words)","As energy prices rise and the climate changes, some people suggest that solar panels should be compulsory on all new buildings. I largely agree with this idea, although there are some difficulties.\n\nThe main argument in favour is that it is much cheaper to install panels while a building is being constructed than to add them later. Furthermore, the owners would save money on their bills for decades, and the country would depend less on imported energy.\n\nHowever, there are also arguments against. Solar panels increase the price of a home, which is already too high for many young people. Besides, they are not equally useful everywhere: a building in the shadow of others will produce very little.\n\nIn conclusion, I think the rule would be positive, provided that there are exceptions for buildings without enough sun and financial help for buyers. In the long term, the benefits clearly outweigh the costs."],
task:["letter","You hired an electrician who did the work badly: some sockets do not work and he left the house dirty. Write a letter of complaint asking for a solution. (about 150 words)","Dear Mr Harris,\n\nI am writing to complain about the electrical work that your company carried out at my home on 3 March.\n\nI hired you to install six new sockets in the kitchen and living room. Unfortunately, two of them do not work at all, and another one becomes hot when I use it, which I find worrying. In addition, your employee left dust and pieces of cable all over the floor, and I had to spend an afternoon cleaning.\n\nI paid the full amount of 480 euros in advance because I trusted your company, so I am very disappointed with the result.\n\nI would therefore like you to send someone to repair the sockets within the next seven days, at no extra cost. If this is not possible, I will expect a partial refund.\n\nI look forward to hearing from you soon.\n\nYours sincerely,\nAlex Martin"]},
C1:{essay:["Is nuclear energy a necessary part of the fight against climate change, or a dangerous distraction? Discuss. (about 200 words)","Few subjects divide environmentalists as sharply as nuclear power. For some it is an indispensable ally against climate change; for others, an expensive relic that diverts resources from better solutions.\n\nThe case in favour rests on one undeniable fact: nuclear plants generate enormous amounts of electricity with almost no carbon emissions, and they do so regardless of the weather. In a system increasingly dependent on wind and sun, such steady output is extremely valuable. Countries that closed their reactors early have frequently ended up burning more gas and coal.\n\nCritics, however, point to cost and time. Recent plants in Europe have taken well over a decade to build and have gone several times over budget. Every euro spent on them, the argument runs, would cut more emissions if it were invested in renewables and storage. The unresolved question of long-term waste adds to their doubts.\n\nOn balance, I believe both sides are partly right. Shutting down existing reactors that operate safely seems to me an unnecessary step backwards. Building large numbers of new ones, on the other hand, is hard to justify while cheaper alternatives are improving so rapidly. Nuclear energy is best seen neither as a saviour nor as a distraction, but as a bridge."],
task:["report","You inspected the electrical installation of an old office building. Write a report for the owner describing its condition and recommending improvements in order of priority. (about 200 words)","Report on the electrical installation at 14 King Street\n\nPurpose\nThis report summarises the findings of the inspection carried out on 10 May and sets out the improvements required, in order of priority.\n\nGeneral condition\nThe installation dates from the 1970s and, although it has been extended several times, it has never been thoroughly renewed. On the whole it no longer meets current regulations.\n\nUrgent issues\nTwo problems require immediate attention. Firstly, several circuits on the second floor have no earth connection, which represents a serious risk of electric shock. Secondly, the main panel shows signs of overheating, probably due to the number of computers and air-conditioning units added in recent years.\n\nOther deficiencies\nThe emergency lighting failed in three of the five corridors tested. In addition, much of the cabling in the basement is exposed and unlabelled, which makes maintenance difficult.\n\nRecommendations\nI strongly recommend replacing the main panel and earthing the affected circuits within the next month. The emergency lighting should be repaired before the next safety inspection. Finally, a complete rewiring of the building ought to be planned within three years; it could be carried out floor by floor to limit disruption."]},
C2:{essay:["'Access to electricity should be considered a human right.' Discuss. (about 220 words)","When the Universal Declaration of Human Rights was drafted in 1948, electricity was a convenience enjoyed by a minority of the world's population. Today it underpins virtually every other entitlement the Declaration proclaims. The question of whether access to it should itself rank as a right is therefore far from frivolous.\n\nThe argument in favour is essentially one of dependency. Without power there is no refrigeration for vaccines, no light by which a child can study after dusk and, increasingly, no access to information or public services. To affirm a right to health or education while remaining indifferent to the means of delivering it is to offer something close to an empty promise.\n\nSceptics object that rights lose their force when they multiply. A right, on the classical view, protects individuals from interference; it does not oblige anyone to provide a commodity that must be generated, transmitted and paid for. Who, precisely, would be in breach when a remote village remains unconnected?\n\nThe objection has weight, yet it is not decisive, since rights to education and to a fair trial also require costly institutions. What matters, to my mind, is less the label than the obligation it creates. Whether or not we call it a right, a world in which hundreds of millions still live in the dark has plainly failed them, and the vocabulary of rights may be the most effective means of saying so."],
task:["proposal","The local council is considering how to make the town's energy supply more resilient. Write a proposal recommending a course of action and justifying it against likely objections. (about 200 words)","Proposal for a resilient local energy supply\n\nBackground\nLast winter's storm left much of the town without power for three days. As extreme weather becomes more frequent, the council can no longer regard such events as exceptional. This proposal recommends a course of action and anticipates the principal objections.\n\nRecommendation\nI propose the creation of a municipal microgrid linking the hospital, the water treatment plant and the two schools designated as emergency shelters. Solar generation on public roofs, coupled with battery storage, would allow these facilities to operate independently for at least seventy-two hours should the main grid fail.\n\nAnticipated objections\nThe most obvious concern is cost. However, a substantial proportion could be met through national resilience funds, and in normal operation the system would reduce the council's own energy bills. It may also be argued that reinforcing the grid is the utility's responsibility. That is true in principle, but burying the affected lines is not planned before 2035, and the town cannot afford to wait.\n\nConclusion\nThe measure would not prevent future outages; it would ensure that, when they occur, the services on which lives depend continue to function. I urge the council to commission a detailed study without delay."]}},
work:{
B1:{essay:["Describe your ideal job. What would you do, where would you work and why would you like it? (about 100 words)","My ideal job would be to work as an engineer in a small company. I would design machines and solve technical problems. I would like to work in an office near my home, so I could walk to work every day.\n\nI would like this job for three reasons. First, I enjoy solving problems. Second, in a small company everybody knows each other and the atmosphere is friendly. Third, I would have time for my family, because I would not need to travel much.\n\nThe salary is important, but for me it is more important to learn new things and to be happy at work."],
task:["email","You saw a job advertisement for a technician. Write an email to the company. Introduce yourself, say why you are interested and ask one question. (about 80 words)","Subject: Technician position\n\nDear Sir or Madam,\n\nI am writing about the technician job that I saw on your website. My name is Alex Martin and I am 28 years old. I studied electricity and I have worked in a factory for three years.\n\nI am interested in this job because I would like to work in a bigger company and learn more.\n\nCould you tell me if the job is full-time?\n\nI attach my CV. Thank you for your time.\n\nYours faithfully,\nAlex Martin"]},
B2:{essay:["'Working from home is better for employees and for companies.' Do you agree? (about 150 words)","Since 2020, working from home has become normal for millions of people. Although it has clear benefits for both workers and companies, I do not think it is the best solution in every case.\n\nOn the positive side, employees save the time and money they used to spend on travelling, and many say they can concentrate better at home. Companies, for their part, need less office space, which reduces their costs.\n\nNevertheless, there are disadvantages too. It is more difficult to learn from colleagues when you only see them on a screen, which is a particular problem for young people in their first job. What is more, some employees find it hard to separate work from their private life and end up working longer hours.\n\nIn my view, the ideal arrangement is a combination of both. Two or three days in the office are enough to keep in touch with the team, while the rest of the week can be spent at home."],
task:["letter","Write a letter of application for the position of team leader in a technical company. Explain your experience and why you are suitable. (about 150 words)","Dear Ms Taylor,\n\nI am writing to apply for the position of team leader, as advertised on your website on 2 June.\n\nI hold a degree in electrical engineering and have been working in the technical office of an installation company for the past two years. My responsibilities include designing electrical projects, supervising work on site and dealing directly with clients. Before that, I spent a year on a production line, where I learned the importance of quality and of working to tight deadlines.\n\nI believe I would be suitable for the role because I am well organised and used to coordinating technicians and suppliers. In my current job I have also trained two new members of staff.\n\nI am particularly attracted by your company's work in renewable energy, a field in which I would very much like to develop my career.\n\nI enclose my CV and would welcome the opportunity to discuss my application at an interview.\n\nYours sincerely,\nAlex Martin"]},
C1:{essay:["Some people believe that job satisfaction matters more than salary. Others think that a good salary is the main purpose of work. Discuss both views and give your opinion. (about 200 words)","Ask people why they work and most will answer, quite reasonably, that they need the money. Yet many of the same people would refuse a well-paid job that they found pointless. This suggests the relationship between pay and satisfaction is more complicated than it first appears.\n\nThose who put salary first make a strong practical case. Work occupies a limited part of life, and a good income buys security, comfort and opportunities for one's family. From this perspective, expecting fulfilment from a job is a luxury, and one that employers sometimes exploit in order to pay less.\n\nOn the other hand, we spend roughly a third of our adult lives at work. Someone who dreads every Monday is unlikely to be compensated by a larger house. Research consistently shows that, beyond the level needed to live comfortably, additional income adds little to happiness, whereas autonomy and a sense of purpose add a great deal.\n\nIn my opinion, the two are not alternatives so much as stages. Until basic needs are securely met, salary inevitably comes first, and it would be naive to pretend otherwise. Once they are, however, I am convinced that the quality of our working days counts for more than the figure on the payslip."],
task:["proposal","Staff morale in your department is low. Write a proposal to your manager identifying the causes and suggesting ways to improve the situation. (about 200 words)","Proposal: improving morale in the technical department\n\nIntroduction\nOver the past six months, three colleagues have left the department and absenteeism has risen noticeably. This proposal identifies the main causes, based on informal conversations with the team, and suggests ways of addressing them.\n\nCauses\nThe most frequent complaint concerns workload. Since the reorganisation, the same number of projects has been shared among fewer people, and overtime has become routine. A second issue is the lack of recognition: staff feel that extra effort goes unnoticed. Finally, several people mentioned that they rarely know how their work contributes to the company's objectives.\n\nSuggestions\nFirst, I would recommend hiring at least one additional technician, even on a temporary contract, to bring overtime back to reasonable levels. Second, a brief monthly meeting in which completed projects are presented would give visible credit to those responsible, at no cost. Third, offering each member of staff a training course of their choice once a year would show that the company is investing in them.\n\nConclusion\nNone of these measures is expensive compared with the cost of replacing experienced staff. I would be happy to discuss them in more detail at your convenience."]},
C2:{essay:["'In the age of artificial intelligence, the most valuable professional skill is the ability to keep learning.' Discuss. (about 220 words)","Every generation of workers has been told that the skills which served their parents will not suffice for them. What distinguishes the present moment is the speed at which that warning is coming true. With artificial intelligence now capable of drafting contracts, writing code and reading scans, it is tempting to conclude that the only durable skill is the capacity to acquire new ones.\n\nThere is much to be said for this view. Specific knowledge has a shorter shelf life than ever, and a professional who mastered a tool five years ago may find it obsolete today. Those who thrive tend to be the ones who treat their expertise as provisional and regard retraining not as a humiliation but as routine.\n\nYet the slogan conceals a difficulty. Learning is not a skill that floats free of content: one learns quickly in a field precisely because one already understands it deeply. The perpetual beginner, moving from one fashionable competence to the next, risks knowing a little about everything and nothing well. Furthermore, judgement, that elusive ability to tell when the machine's confident answer is wrong, is the product of long experience rather than agility.\n\nI would therefore amend the statement. The ability to keep learning is indispensable, but it is valuable only when anchored in genuine mastery of something. Adaptability without depth is merely restlessness."],
task:["report","You were asked to evaluate a pilot scheme in which your department worked a four-day week for six months. Write a report on the results, including unexpected findings, and make recommendations. (about 200 words)","Report on the four-day week pilot scheme\n\nIntroduction\nThis report evaluates the pilot conducted in the design department between January and June, during which staff worked thirty-two hours over four days with no reduction in pay.\n\nResults\nContrary to initial fears, output did not decline. The number of projects delivered was marginally higher than in the same period last year, and the proportion requiring correction fell from 9% to 6%. Absenteeism dropped by almost half, while every member of staff expressed a wish to continue.\n\nUnexpected findings\nTwo outcomes had not been anticipated. Meetings, which were cut back to protect productive time, proved largely dispensable, suggesting that many had been unnecessary all along. Less positively, clients occasionally struggled to reach their usual contact on Fridays, and two complaints were received. Some staff also reported that the four working days had become noticeably more intense.\n\nRecommendations\nIn light of these results, I recommend making the arrangement permanent, subject to two conditions. A rota should ensure that at least two engineers are available every Friday, and workloads should be monitored quarterly to prevent the compressed week from becoming a source of stress in its own right. Extending the scheme to other departments merits serious consideration."]}},
daily:{
B1:{essay:["Describe your typical day. What do you do in the morning, in the afternoon and in the evening? (about 100 words)","On a typical day I get up at half past six. I have a shower and I drink a coffee, but I do not usually have breakfast. Then I drive to work. I start at eight o'clock.\n\nIn the afternoon I have lunch with my colleagues at one, and I finish work at five. After work I sometimes go to the supermarket or to the gym.\n\nIn the evening I cook dinner with my wife and we talk about our day. After dinner we watch a series or I study English for half an hour. I usually go to bed at eleven, because I get up early."],
task:["email","A friend from another country is going to visit your city. Write an email. Suggest two places to visit and say what the weather is like. (about 80 words)","Subject: Your visit!\n\nHi Tom,\n\nI'm so happy that you are coming in May! I want to show you my city.\n\nFirst, we can visit the old town. It has small streets and very good restaurants. Second, we can take a boat to the islands. The beaches are beautiful.\n\nThe weather in May is usually nice, but it sometimes rains, so bring a jacket.\n\nTell me what time your plane arrives and I will meet you at the airport.\n\nSee you soon,\nAlex"]},
B2:{essay:["'Life in the city is better than life in the countryside.' Do you agree? (about 150 words)","Many young people dream of moving to a big city, while others would love to escape from one. Personally, I believe that each option has its advantages, and the best choice depends on the stage of life you are in.\n\nThere is no doubt that cities offer more opportunities. It is easier to find a job, there are universities and hospitals nearby, and you can go to the cinema or a concert whenever you like. Public transport also means that you do not need a car.\n\nIn contrast, life in the countryside is quieter and healthier. The air is cleaner, houses are cheaper and people tend to know their neighbours. On the other hand, services are limited, and young people may feel bored or isolated.\n\nAll things considered, I think the city is better when you are starting your career, whereas the countryside is ideal for bringing up children or for retirement."],
task:["review","Write a review of a restaurant, café or shop in your area for a local website. Describe it, say what you liked and disliked, and say whether you recommend it. (about 150 words)","Café Luna: a hidden gem\n\nIf you are looking for a quiet place to have breakfast in the city centre, Café Luna is an excellent choice. It opened last year in a small street behind the market, and it is easy to miss.\n\nThe first thing you notice is the atmosphere. The café is decorated with plants and old wooden furniture, and there is soft music in the background. The coffee is the best I have had in town, and the homemade cakes are delicious, especially the carrot cake. The staff are friendly and remember regular customers.\n\nThe only negative point is the size. There are just eight tables, so at weekends you often have to wait. It is also slightly more expensive than other cafés nearby.\n\nNevertheless, I would definitely recommend it, particularly on weekday mornings. It is the perfect place to read or to meet a friend."]},
C1:{essay:["Many people today complain that they never have enough time. What are the causes of this feeling, and what can be done about it? (about 200 words)","It is one of the paradoxes of modern life that, although we own more time-saving devices than any generation before us, we constantly complain of having no time. The causes of this feeling are worth examining, because they point to possible remedies.\n\nOne explanation is that technology has blurred the boundary between work and leisure. When a message from the office can arrive at any hour, we are never completely off duty, even if we are not actually working. A second cause is the sheer number of options available to us. Faced with endless series, courses and activities, we are left with the impression that we are always missing something.\n\nThere is also a social dimension. Being busy has become a sign of importance, so that admitting to a free afternoon almost feels like a confession of failure.\n\nAs for solutions, I doubt that better organisation is the answer, since most of us already plan our days in detail. What is needed, rather, is the willingness to do less. Setting firm limits on when we can be contacted, and deliberately leaving some hours unplanned, would do more for our sense of time than any new application. In short, the problem is not a shortage of hours but an excess of demands."],
task:["letter","A new bar has opened under your flat and the noise at night is unbearable. Write a letter to the local council explaining the situation and asking them to take action. (about 200 words)","Dear Sir or Madam,\n\nI am writing on behalf of the residents of 25 Harbour Street to draw your attention to the serious disturbance caused by the bar which opened on the ground floor of our building in March.\n\nSince its opening, the premises have remained open until well after three in the morning from Thursday to Sunday. Music can be clearly heard in the flats above, and customers gather in the street to smoke and talk, which makes sleep virtually impossible. Several residents, among them families with young children and an elderly couple, have seen their health affected.\n\nWe have approached the owner on three occasions. Although he was polite and promised to reduce the volume, nothing has changed. We have also called the local police twice, without lasting results.\n\nWe understand that the bar's licence permits it to open only until one o'clock and requires it to be soundproofed. We would therefore ask the council to inspect the premises and to ensure that these conditions are enforced.\n\nWe have no wish to see a local business close, but we believe we are entitled to rest in our own homes. I enclose a list of signatures and look forward to your reply.\n\nYours faithfully,\nAlex Martin"]},
C2:{essay:["'We know more about distant strangers than about our own neighbours.' What does this say about modern life? (about 220 words)","It is a curious feature of contemporary life that one may follow, in real time, the breakfast habits of an acquaintance on another continent while remaining ignorant of the name of the person on the other side of the wall. The observation has become something of a cliché, but clichés generally earn their status by being true.\n\nThe explanation lies partly in technology and partly in the way we now choose our company. Earlier generations had little alternative to those who happened to live nearby; community was a matter of geography. Today we assemble our social world according to shared interests, and distance is no obstacle. In many respects this is a liberation. Nobody who has felt out of place in a small town will lament the freedom to find like-minded people elsewhere.\n\nSomething has nevertheless been mislaid in the exchange. A neighbour is, by definition, someone we did not select, and learning to rub along with such people was once an everyday education in tolerance. Online, by contrast, we can simply dismiss anyone who irritates us. Nor can a distant friend, however dear, lend a ladder or notice that our shutters have stayed closed for three days.\n\nWhat the phenomenon reveals, I think, is that we have exchanged communities of fate for communities of choice. The gain in freedom is real, yet so is the loss in solidarity, and we would be wise not to pretend otherwise."],
task:["review","Write a review of a film, series or book that offers an interesting portrait of everyday life. Analyse how it achieves this and whether it succeeds. (about 200 words)","'Paterson', directed by Jim Jarmusch\n\nNothing happens in 'Paterson', and that is precisely the point. Jim Jarmusch's film follows a week in the life of a bus driver who shares his name with the New Jersey town where he lives. He gets up, drives his route, walks the dog, drinks one beer and, in the gaps, writes poetry in a notebook that nobody reads.\n\nWhat might have been unbearably dull becomes, in Jarmusch's hands, quietly hypnotic. The repetition is the method: because each day follows the same pattern, the smallest variation, an overheard conversation or a box of matches on the kitchen table, acquires the weight of an event. Adam Driver's wonderfully restrained performance suggests a man who is not resigned to routine but nourished by it.\n\nThe film is not flawless. The protagonist's wife, forever redecorating in black and white, is closer to a charming idea than a person, and some viewers will find the pace self-indulgent.\n\nYet as a portrait of ordinary life, it succeeds where more dramatic films fail, for it grasps that most existence is made of exactly these unremarkable days. It will not suit those in search of plot. For anyone prepared to slow down, it offers the rare experience of seeing the everyday made luminous."]}},
cook:{
B1:{essay:["Write about your favourite meal. What is it? Who cooks it? When do you eat it? (about 100 words)","My favourite meal is roast chicken with potatoes. My mother cooks it, and she has made it since I was a child. It is a simple meal, but nobody makes it like her.\n\nShe puts lemon, garlic and herbs inside the chicken and cooks it in the oven for more than one hour. The potatoes are cooked in the same dish, so they are full of flavour.\n\nWe usually eat it on Sundays, when all the family is together. After lunch we drink coffee and talk for hours. When I eat this meal, I feel like I am at home."],
task:["email","You want to invite some friends to dinner at your house. Write an email. Say when it is, what you will cook and ask if they have any food problems. (about 80 words)","Subject: Dinner on Saturday\n\nHi everyone,\n\nI would like to invite you to dinner at my house next Saturday at eight o'clock.\n\nI am going to cook pasta with seafood, and there will be a chocolate cake for dessert. You do not need to bring anything, but if you want, you can bring something to drink.\n\nIs there any food that you cannot eat? Please tell me before Thursday.\n\nI hope you can all come!\n\nBest wishes,\nAlex"]},
B2:{essay:["'Young people today do not know how to cook.' Do you agree? What could be done about it? (about 150 words)","It is often claimed that young people nowadays are unable to cook and survive on pizza and ready meals. In my opinion, there is some truth in this, although the situation is not as bad as people think.\n\nIt is true that many young adults cook less than their parents did. They work or study long hours, and ordering food on their phones is fast and convenient. In addition, many of them were never taught to cook at home.\n\nHowever, it would be unfair to generalise. Thanks to the internet, a large number of young people are interested in food and learn recipes from videos. Some of them cook dishes from all over the world, which their grandparents never tried.\n\nAs for solutions, I think schools should offer basic cooking lessons, since it is a skill for life. Parents could also ask their children to help in the kitchen from an early age."],
task:["review","Write a review of a restaurant where you ate recently. Describe the food, the service and the prices, and say whether you would recommend it. (about 150 words)","La Brasa: great meat, slow service\n\nLast Saturday I had dinner at La Brasa, a grill restaurant that opened a few months ago near the harbour. I had heard good things about it, so my expectations were high.\n\nThe food did not disappoint. We ordered a mixed grill for two, which came with roast peppers and homemade chips. The meat was tender and perfectly cooked, and the portions were generous. The desserts were less impressive: my cheesecake was rather dry.\n\nUnfortunately, the service was not at the same level. Although the waiters were polite, we waited forty minutes for our main course, and nobody explained the delay. The restaurant was full, and there were clearly not enough staff.\n\nAs for the price, we paid thirty euros each, which is reasonable for the quality.\n\nOverall, I would recommend La Brasa to meat lovers, but go on a weekday, when it is probably quieter."]},
C1:{essay:["Traditional home cooking is disappearing in many countries. Why is this happening, and does it matter? (about 200 words)","In many countries, the dishes that grandparents prepared from memory are being replaced by ready meals and delivery services. This change has several causes, and I believe it matters more than is generally recognised.\n\nThe most obvious reason is lack of time. Traditional recipes often demand hours of slow cooking, which is difficult to reconcile with long working days and commuting. A second factor is that the chain of transmission has been broken: when both parents work, children no longer learn by watching, and recipes that were never written down are simply lost. Convenience has done the rest, since food of acceptable quality can now be delivered to the door in half an hour.\n\nDoes this matter? In terms of nutrition, it clearly does, since industrial food tends to contain far more salt, sugar and fat than its homemade equivalent. But there is also a cultural loss. A cuisine is a form of memory, and families that cook together pass on far more than recipes.\n\nI do not think the answer is to feel nostalgic about a past in which women spent their lives in the kitchen. Rather, cooking should be taught as a basic skill to everyone, so that tradition survives as a shared pleasure and not as an obligation."],
task:["proposal","Your company canteen is unpopular: staff complain about the quality and variety of the food. Write a proposal to the management suggesting improvements. (about 200 words)","Proposal: improving the staff canteen\n\nIntroduction\nA recent survey showed that only 30% of employees use the canteen regularly, and that most of those who do are dissatisfied. This proposal summarises the main complaints and puts forward three improvements.\n\nCurrent problems\nThe criticism most often mentioned was the lack of variety: the same six dishes are repeated every week. Many respondents also felt that the food was heavy and offered few healthy alternatives. Finally, vegetarians pointed out that there is rarely a suitable main course.\n\nSuggested improvements\nFirstly, I suggest introducing a four-week rotating menu, which would greatly reduce repetition without increasing costs. Secondly, a salad bar could be installed, allowing staff to prepare a light meal quickly. Thirdly, at least one vegetarian main dish ought to be available every day.\n\nIn addition, it would be worth inviting the catering company to hold a tasting session twice a year, so that staff could vote on new dishes.\n\nConclusion\nThese changes would require only a modest investment and would encourage more employees to eat on site, which in turn would make the service more profitable. I would be glad to help organise a trial period."]},
C2:{essay:["'What we eat is no longer a private choice but a moral and political act.' Discuss. (about 220 words)","There was a time when the contents of one's plate were nobody's business but one's own. That time has passed. Whether we buy battery eggs, eat beef or throw away half a loaf, we are now told that we are making an ethical statement, and one with consequences far beyond our own kitchens.\n\nThe argument is difficult to refute on the facts. Agriculture accounts for a considerable share of greenhouse emissions, and the difference between diets is large enough to matter. Nor is it only a question of climate: the conditions in which animals are raised and labourers are paid are, in a real sense, chosen at the checkout. To insist that eating is purely private is to ignore everyone else who is affected by it.\n\nAnd yet there is something uncomfortable about the moralisation of the dinner table. The freedom to choose 'ethically' is unevenly distributed, and it is all too easy for those with money and leisure to lecture those with neither. Moreover, the emphasis on individual virtue conveniently distracts attention from the subsidies and regulations that shape what is available in the first place.\n\nMy own view is that food is indeed a moral matter, but that the morality is collective before it is personal. A society that makes the harmful option the cheapest one can hardly blame its poorer members for choosing it."],
task:["review","Write a review of a restaurant with an ambitious, innovative approach. Assess whether the innovation serves the food or the chef's ego. (about 200 words)","Alquimia: brilliance, with a side of showmanship\n\nA meal at Alquimia begins not with a menu but with a manifesto, delivered by a waiter who informs you that the chef 'does not serve dishes but provokes memories'. One's heart sinks. Happily, much of what follows is considerably better than the introduction.\n\nThe opening courses are little short of dazzling. A transparent 'ravioli' of tomato water bursts on the tongue with the concentrated essence of August, and a deceptively plain bowl of rice, cooked in a stock of roasted fish bones, is the most deeply satisfying thing I have eaten this year. Here technique is the servant of flavour, as it should be.\n\nElsewhere, the balance tips the other way. A dessert served on a pillow that releases the scent of pine as it deflates is memorable for all the wrong reasons, while a course eaten blindfolded tells us more about the chef's opinion of himself than about the ingredients.\n\nNine of the fourteen courses are superb, which at these prices ought perhaps to be fourteen. Alquimia is at its best when it forgets to be clever. I would return without hesitation, provided someone had first had a quiet word about the blindfolds."]}},
foot:{
B1:{essay:["Write about a sport you like. How often do you play or watch it? Why do you like it? (about 100 words)","My favourite sport is football. I have liked it since I was a child, because my father and my brother love it too.\n\nNow I do not play very often, only once or twice a month with my friends from work. We play on a small pitch near the office. But I watch football every weekend on TV, and sometimes I go to the stadium.\n\nI like football because it is exciting. A match can change in one minute. I also like it because it is a team sport: one player cannot win alone. For me, football is the best way to relax."],
task:["email","Your football team needs a new player. Write an email to a friend. Invite him to join, and say when and where you play. (about 80 words)","Subject: Join our team!\n\nHi Luis,\n\nHow are you? I am writing because my football team needs a new player. One of our friends moved to another city last month.\n\nWe play every Wednesday at eight in the evening, at the sports centre next to the station. The team is not very good, but we always have fun. After the match we usually have dinner together.\n\nWould you like to join us? You only need to bring your boots.\n\nWrite soon,\nAlex"]},
B2:{essay:["'Professional footballers are paid too much.' Do you agree? (about 150 words)","Every summer, newspapers publish the astonishing salaries of the world's best footballers, and many people react with anger. Although I understand this reaction, I do not completely agree with it.\n\nIt certainly seems unfair that a player earns more in a week than a nurse or a teacher does in several years, especially as these professions are far more useful to society. Furthermore, such enormous amounts of money can have a negative effect on very young players.\n\nHowever, we should remember where the money comes from. Footballers are not paid by taxpayers but by clubs, which earn millions from television, advertising and tickets. If the players did not receive that money, it would simply stay with the owners. Besides, a career in football is short, and only a tiny minority become rich.\n\nIn conclusion, I think the salaries are a consequence of the huge interest in the sport. If we really believe they are too high, we should stop paying to watch."],
task:["report","Your local sports club wants to attract more young members. Write a report for the committee describing the current situation and suggesting ways to attract them. (about 150 words)","Report: attracting young members to the club\n\nIntroduction\nThe purpose of this report is to describe the current situation regarding young members and to suggest ways of increasing their number.\n\nCurrent situation\nAt present, only 15% of our members are under eighteen, compared with 40% ten years ago. According to several parents I spoke to, the main reasons are the cost of membership and the fact that training takes place late in the evening.\n\nSuggestions\nFirst of all, the club could offer a reduced fee for under-eighteens, as well as a discount for families with two or more children. Secondly, an extra training session should be organised on Saturday mornings. Finally, it would be a good idea to visit local schools and hold an open day, so that children can try the sport for free.\n\nConclusion\nI believe these measures would make the club more attractive to young people without requiring a large investment."]},
C1:{essay:["Major sporting events such as the World Cup cost host countries enormous sums of money. Are they worth it? (about 200 words)","Whenever a country is chosen to host a World Cup or the Olympic Games, its politicians promise jobs, tourists and a lasting legacy. The bill, however, regularly runs into billions, and it is fair to ask whether the investment is justified.\n\nSupporters point to the benefits. Hosting a major event forces governments to build infrastructure, such as airports and public transport, that might otherwise be postponed for decades. The country enjoys weeks of worldwide publicity, and there is an undeniable boost to national pride.\n\nThe evidence, however, is less encouraging. Economists who have studied past events have generally found that the promised growth fails to appear. Tourists who would have come anyway stay away to avoid the crowds, and many of the jobs created are temporary. Worst of all are the stadiums built for a month of football and left to decay afterwards.\n\nIn my view, these events are worth holding only under strict conditions. A country that can use existing stadiums and already has good infrastructure may well benefit. For one that has to build everything from scratch, on the other hand, the money would almost certainly do more good if it were spent on schools and hospitals. The party is wonderful, but someone has to pay for it."],
task:["letter","Your football club has announced a large increase in ticket prices. As a long-standing supporter, write a letter to the club's board expressing your concerns and suggesting alternatives. (about 200 words)","Dear Members of the Board,\n\nI have been a season-ticket holder for twenty-two years, and I am writing to express my deep concern about the 35% rise in ticket prices announced last week.\n\nI appreciate that the club faces rising costs and that the new stand must be paid for. Nevertheless, an increase of this size, introduced without any consultation, will make it impossible for many loyal supporters to continue attending. Families and pensioners, who have stood by the team through three relegations, are likely to be the first to disappear.\n\nI would also suggest that the measure may prove counterproductive. A half-empty stadium damages the atmosphere on which the team depends, and reduces the income from food, drink and merchandise.\n\nMay I therefore propose some alternatives? The increase could be introduced gradually over three seasons. Reduced prices might be kept for those who have held a season ticket for over ten years, as well as for under-eighteens. Additional revenue could be sought from corporate seats instead.\n\nSupporters are not merely customers; we are the reason the club exists. I would urge the board to reconsider, and I would welcome the opportunity for supporters' representatives to discuss the matter with you.\n\nYours faithfully,\nAlex Martin"]},
C2:{essay:["'Modern football has more in common with the entertainment industry than with sport.' Discuss. (about 220 words)","The charge that football has sold its soul is almost as old as professionalism itself, but it has rarely seemed more plausible than now. With clubs owned by investment funds and sovereign states, and fixtures scheduled to suit broadcasters on the far side of the globe, the game increasingly resembles a branch of show business.\n\nThe similarities are hard to deny. Leading players are marketed as celebrities whose value lies as much in their following on social media as in their performances. Clubs tour distant continents in the summer, much as rock bands do, and the proposed breakaway leagues of recent years openly borrowed their logic from television: guaranteed stars, guaranteed audiences, no risk of failure.\n\nAnd yet the comparison breaks down at the crucial point. A film has a script; a match does not. However much money is spent, the result cannot be bought with certainty, and it is precisely the possibility that the giant may stumble which gives sport its hold over us. Significantly, it was this that supporters rose to defend when the breakaway was announced, and their revolt succeeded.\n\nI would conclude that football is an entertainment industry built around something that is not, at heart, entertainment. Its commercial masters would do well to remember the distinction. Remove the uncertainty, and what remains is merely a very expensive form of theatre."],
task:["review","Write a review of a sports book, documentary or film. Consider what it reveals about sport beyond the action itself. (about 200 words)","'Diego Maradona', directed by Asif Kapadia\n\nSports documentaries tend to follow a weary formula: triumph, setback, redemption. Asif Kapadia's film about Maradona's years in Naples is a far darker and more interesting proposition.\n\nAssembled entirely from archive footage, much of it never previously seen, it traces the seven years in which a poor, despised city adopted a footballer as its saviour and then, almost as abruptly, turned on him. Kapadia's central insight is the distinction between 'Diego', the shy boy from the slums, and 'Maradona', the character he invented in order to survive the adoration. The tragedy is that the invention eventually swallowed the man.\n\nThe football itself is electrifying, yet it is not really the subject. What the film reveals is the appalling weight we place on those we elevate, and how readily worship curdles into resentment once the idol ceases to deliver.\n\nIf there is a weakness, it lies in the final act, which hurries through the decades of decline as though embarrassed by them. Some will also wish for a more critical account of his conduct towards others.\n\nEven so, this is essential viewing, and not only for supporters. It is less a film about sport than a study of what fame does to a human being."]}}
};

// Help shown by the "?" button: structure + tips per task type
DATA.help = {
essay:{title:"Essay",structure:["Introduction: present the topic and (optionally) your position","Paragraph 1: first main idea with a reason or example","Paragraph 2: second idea or the opposite view","Conclusion: summarise and give your opinion clearly"],tips:["Use linking words: however, moreover, on the other hand, in conclusion","One idea per paragraph","Don't introduce new ideas in the conclusion","Keep a neutral or formal tone; avoid contractions at C1–C2"]},
email:{title:"Email",structure:["Subject line","Greeting (Dear… / Hi…)","Why you are writing","Details, one point per short paragraph","What you expect next + closing (Best regards / See you soon)"],tips:["Match the tone to the reader: formal for a boss, friendly for a friend","Answer every point in the task","Keep paragraphs short"]},
letter:{title:"Formal letter",structure:["Dear Sir or Madam / Dear Mr… ","Reason for writing (I am writing to…)","Background and details in separate paragraphs","What you want to happen","Closing: I look forward to hearing from you + Yours faithfully (no name known) / Yours sincerely (name known)"],tips:["No contractions","Be firm but polite when complaining","Useful phrases: I would be grateful if…, I would therefore ask you to…"]},
report:{title:"Report",structure:["Title","Introduction: the aim of the report","Sections with headings (findings, advantages, problems…)","Conclusion / Recommendations"],tips:["Use headings","Be factual and impersonal: It was found that…","Passive voice is common","End with a clear recommendation"]},
proposal:{title:"Proposal",structure:["Title","Introduction: the current situation and the aim","Suggestions, each under its own heading, with benefits","Conclusion: persuade the reader to act"],tips:["A proposal looks to the future (a report describes the past)","Use persuasive language: I would recommend…, This would allow us to…","Anticipate objections such as cost"]},
review:{title:"Review",structure:["Catchy title","Introduction: what you are reviewing","Description (don't tell the whole story)","What you liked and disliked","Recommendation: who is it for?"],tips:["Use rich adjectives and adverbs","Give your opinion with reasons","Present tenses are usual when describing plots"]},
q:{title:"Answering a question",structure:["Answer directly in the first sentence","Give a reason","Add an example or detail","Finish with a short closing idea"],tips:["Don't answer with one sentence: develop it","Use the tense of the question","It's fine to take a second to think: 'Well, let me see…'"]},
describe:{title:"Describing an image",structure:["General idea: This picture shows…","Details: in the foreground / background, on the left / right","Speculate: he might be…, it looks as if…","Personal reaction or opinion"],tips:["Use present continuous for actions: she is repairing…","Use modals to speculate: must, might, could","Don't just list objects: connect them"]},
compare:{title:"Comparing two images",structure:["What both pictures have in common","Main differences: whereas, while, in contrast","Answer the question you were asked","Your own preference, with a reason"],tips:["Don't describe each picture separately; compare them","Useful: Both pictures show…, The first one… whereas the second…","Speak for about one minute"]},
mono:{title:"One-minute talk",structure:["Introduce the topic in one sentence","Tell it in order: first, then, after that","Give details and feelings","End with what you learned or think now"],tips:["Use past tenses for stories","Use time linkers: at first, eventually, in the end","Keep going: if you forget a word, explain it with others"]},
op:{title:"Giving an opinion",structure:["State your position: I (partly) agree because…","First argument with an example","Mention the other side: however, on the other hand","Conclude: so, on balance…"],tips:["It's fine to agree only partly","Useful: As far as I'm concerned…, I'd argue that…","Support every opinion with a reason"]},
rp:{title:"Role-play",structure:["Greet and explain who you are / why you are speaking","Explain the situation clearly","Ask for or offer a solution","Close politely"],tips:["Be polite: Could you…?, I'm afraid…, Would it be possible…?","Adapt the tone to the situation (boss, client, friend)","Stay in the role"]}
};
